    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The apt install failed because there's not enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives (needs 101MB). I need to check disk space and free up space before installing. I could also try to clean apt caches or find an alternative way to install just gcc/gcov without the full build-essential which pulls in many large packages.",
  "plan": "Check available disk space with df. Then try apt-get clean to clear cached archives, and check what's consuming space. I might also install a more minimal set of packages (gcc, gcov, make) rather than full build-essential to reduce download size.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives 2>/dev/null; ls -la /var/cache/apt/archives | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The root filesystem is 100% full (984G total, 960G used, 0 available). The apt cache is empty, so the problem is elsewhere. I need to find and remove large files to free up space for the installation.",
  "plan": "Find large files/directories consuming space. I'll check common culprits like /var, /usr, /tmp, and look for large files. Then clean up to free enough space to install the toolchain.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -rh | head -20\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Strange situation: `df` reports the root filesystem is 100% full (984G total, 960G used, 0 available), but `du` only accounts for ~180M of actual files. This suggests the 960G 'used' is from the host's underlying disk shared via fuse-overlayfs, while the container's visible files are tiny. I need to understand the real constraint before installing. Let me check mount details and test actual writability.",
  "plan": "Examine /proc/mounts to understand the overlay setup, test whether I can actually write a small file, and check for any deleted-but-open files. This determines whether the space issue is real or just a reporting artifact. If I can write small files, I may be able to work around apt's space check.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /proc/mounts | grep -E 'overlay|/ ' \n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "touch /test_write && echo 'write OK' && rm /test_write\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /tmp /var /app\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

The disk-usage reports are misleading — `df` shows 100% from the host's shared disk, but a file write succeeded. Let me verify actual write capacity and try to work around apt's conservative space check.

{
  "analysis": "This is a fuse-overlayfs mount where the 'Used/Avail' reflects the host's underlying disk (984G, 959G used, 0 avail). A small file write succeeded, so the real constraint may just be apt's conservative space check. I need to test whether I can actually write substantial data, and if so, bypass apt's space check (or free space).",
  "plan": "Test writing a ~50MB file to confirm real write capacity. If it works, apt's space check is the only blocker — I'll override it using apt's -o Dpkg::NoTriggers or by setting the config to ignore the disk check. Actually the relevant option is to just proceed; apt checks at download time. I'll first test write capacity, then attempt install with config to disable the free-space check (apt won't have a direct flag, but the dpkg side can proceed). Let me verify with dd first.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/testfile bs=1M count=50 2>&1; df -h / ; rm -f /tmp/testfile\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: writing a 50MB file succeeded even though `df` reports 0 available. This is a fuse-overlayfs mount where statvfs reports the host's reserved-space-excluded free blocks (0), but root can still write into the ~5% reserved space (~49GB). So apt's disk-space pre-check is the only blocker — actual writes work. I'll attempt the install and, if apt refuses on the space check, fall back to downloading the debs and installing with dpkg (which doesn't enforce the same check).",
  "plan": "First, attempt a minimal install of just `gcc` and `make` (smaller than full build-essential) to confirm whether apt's space check blocks. Based on the result, I'll either let it proceed or pivot to downloading the .deb files and installing via dpkg as root.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install -y gcc make 2>&1 | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 10.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt's disk-space pre-check is the only blocker — actual file writes succeed (root can use reserved blocks even though statvfs reports 0 available). I need an alternative way to obtain the gcc/make/gcov packages and install them. Options: (1) get the .deb URLs via apt and download them with another tool, then `dpkg -i` (dpkg doesn't enforce the same space check), or (2) mount a tmpfs over the apt cache so apt sees real free space. First I need to know what download tools are available (python3, perl, busybox, nc) and how much RAM is present.",
  "plan": "Check for python3, perl, busybox, and free RAM. If python3 is present, I'll use apt-get --print-uris to list the .deb URLs, download them with python, then dpkg -i. That fully avoids apt's space check. I'll also generate the uri list as a fallback.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which python3 python perl busybox nc curl 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; free -m\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

